  "notes": "West leads the ♠Q against 6NT which South wins. South has 2 spade tricks, 4 heart tricks, 2 diamond tricks, and 3 club tricks. South is 1 trick short and must look towards clubs for trick number 12.\n\nIf clubs are 3-3 or 4-2, South can easily succeed by cashing the ♣A, ♣K, ♣Q and conceding a club trick if necessary. South will have his twelfth trick established in clubs.\n\nSouth should turn his attention to the possibility of a 5-1 club break. South's best play is to lead a low club towards the ♣10 in dummy on the first round of the suit. If West has 5 clubs to the ♣J or a singleton ♣J, dummy's ♣10 will be good for trick number 12.\n\nIf it turns out that clubs were 4-2 or 3-3, South may lose an unnecessary club trick, but the contract will still be secure.",
